{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790514445,"nonce":"vdd7BDrhG3hazLqYdPW/A0tnUkA=","title":"The caribou library's own natives in a compiled program","message":"In a wasm program, a Haxe call to the caribou library's own natives raises \"Native library 'caribou' not loaded, or it exports no ...\". Examples: new caribou.Future(), Future.await/resolve/reject/ready, and a plugin's Future result (math Data.later). In hosted runs these are the adapter's host natives, generic dispatchers (import.rs Kind::FutureNew/FutureAwait/...), which can't be linked ahead of time. They need link-shaped entry points in caribou-ash (caribou_haxe_future_new, ..._await and so on) and a driver link table entry for each native, plus a TypeTag::FUTURE cast for plugin results.\n\nRepro: crates/caribou-interop/fixtures/src/UseData.hx's futures() in a wasm project (see caribou-driver tests/linked.rs data_program, which runs data() only). await in a compiled program also has to suspend a fiber on wasm, as Ash's fibers do over Workers.","files":null}]}